tests.prompt:
Classify this Express handler. Assume req.query.email is attacker-controlled and db.query executes PostgreSQL text exactly as supplied.

const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const result = await db.query(
  'SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE email = \' ' + email + ' \' '
);
res.json(result.rows);

If vulnerable, return exactly: VERDICT: VULNERABLE; CWE: CWE-N
If safe, return exactly: VERDICT: SAFE

Classify this Express handler. Assume req.query.email is attacker-controlled and db.query implements PostgreSQL parameter binding for $1.

const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const result = await db.query(
  'SELECT id, email FROM users WHERE email = $1',
  [email]
);
res.json(result.rows);

If vulnerable, return exactly: VERDICT: VULNERABLE; CWE: CWE-N
If safe, return exactly: VERDICT: SAFE

Choose the only correct exploit-path trace for this handler.

const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const sql = 'SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = \' ' + email + ' \' ';
const result = await db.query(sql);
res.json(result.rows);

OPTION_A: res.json is the attacker-controlled source; result.rows modifies the SQL statement; db.query is only a formatter.
OPTION_B: req.query.email is attacker-controlled; concatenation changes SQL syntax; db.query executes the resulting statement.
OPTION_C: The static SELECT keyword is attacker-controlled; String blocks metacharacters; res.json executes the query.

Return exactly one token: EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_A, EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_B, or EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_C

Choose the only patch that prevents SQL injection while preserving lookup by arbitrary email address.

Vulnerable code:
const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const rows = await db.query('SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = \' ' + email + ' \' ');

OPTION_A:
const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const rows = await db.query('SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]);

OPTION_B:
const email = String(req.query.email ?? '').replace("'", '');
const rows = await db.query('SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = \' ' + email + ' \' ');

OPTION_C:
const email = String(req.query.email ?? '');
const rows = await db.query('SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = \' ' + email + ' \' ');
if (email.includes("'")) throw new Error('invalid');

Return exactly one token: PATCH: OPTION_A, PATCH: OPTION_B, or PATCH: OPTION_C

Classify this Express handler. Assume req.body.archive is attacker-controlled, folder is the fixed string /srv/reports, and exec invokes /bin/sh.

const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
const folder = '/srv/reports';
exec('tar -czf /tmp/' + archive + '.tgz ' + folder, callback);

If vulnerable, return exactly: VERDICT: VULNERABLE; CWE: CWE-N
If safe, return exactly: VERDICT: SAFE

Classify this Express handler. Assume execFile invokes tar directly without a shell and folder is the fixed string /srv/reports.

const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
if (!/^[a-z0-9_-]{1,40}$/i.test(archive)) {
  return res.status(400).end();
}
const folder = '/srv/reports';
execFile('tar', ['-czf', '/tmp/' + archive + '.tgz', folder], callback);

If vulnerable, return exactly: VERDICT: VULNERABLE; CWE: CWE-N
If safe, return exactly: VERDICT: SAFE

Choose the only correct exploit-path trace for this handler. Assume exec invokes /bin/sh.

const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
const command = 'tar -czf /tmp/' + archive + '.tgz /srv/reports';
exec(command, callback);

OPTION_A: callback controls archive; tar sanitizes shell syntax; req.body is only used after execution.
OPTION_B: The fixed /srv/reports path is attacker-controlled; String executes shell syntax; callback launches tar.
OPTION_C: req.body.archive is attacker-controlled; concatenation places it in shell command text; exec asks the shell to interpret it.

Return exactly one token: EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_A, EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_B, or EXPLOIT_PATH: OPTION_C

Choose the only patch that prevents command injection while still creating a named tar archive.

Vulnerable code:
const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
exec('tar -czf /tmp/' + archive + '.tgz /srv/reports', callback);

OPTION_A:
const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
if (!/^[a-z0-9_-]{1,40}$/i.test(archive)) return res.status(400).end();
execFile('tar', ['-czf', '/tmp/' + archive + '.tgz', '/srv/reports'], callback);

OPTION_B:
const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '').replace(';', '');
exec('tar -czf /tmp/' + archive + '.tgz /srv/reports', callback);

OPTION_C:
const archive = String(req.body.archive ?? '');
exec('tar -czf /tmp/' + archive + '.tgz /srv/reports', callback);
if (/[;&|]/.test(archive)) return res.status(400).end();

Return exactly one token: PATCH: OPTION_A, PATCH: OPTION_B, or PATCH: OPTION_C
